---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevFormatJsonCode from '@/components/tools/dev/DevFormatJsonCode.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="json-to-json-schema">
  <DevFormatJsonCode client:load target="json-schema" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>What JSON Schema is for</h2>
    <p>
      JSON Schema describes the shape of JSON in a language-independent way, so the same document works
      everywhere: Ajv in Node, python-jsonschema, Go&rsquo;s santhosh-tekuri/jsonschema, OpenAPI request
      and response definitions, VS Code&rsquo;s editor completion for config files, and form generators
      such as JSON Forms and RJSF. To write one by hand for an existing payload is slow. To generate the
      JSON Schema skeleton from a sample and then refine it is much faster.
    </p>

    <h2>Draft 2020-12</h2>
    <p>
      The output declares <code>$schema: "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema"</code>. That is
      the current release and the default in Ajv 8 and modern Python tooling. For an earlier draft, the
      differences are small: draft-07 uses <code>definitions</code> in place of <code>$defs</code>, and
      an array-valued <code>items</code> in place of <code>prefixItems</code> for tuple typing. Output from
      a plain sample has neither.
    </p>

    <h2>The inference rules</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Whole numbers become <code>"type": "integer"</code>, fractional ones <code>"number"</code>. A field that was sometimes each becomes <code>"number"</code>.</li>
      <li>A key present in all merged samples of an object goes into <code>required</code>. A key present in only some does not.</li>
      <li>Each object gets <code>additionalProperties: false</code>, because a schema from a sample should be strict by default. An unexpected key is the drift you want to catch.</li>
      <li>Arrays get an <code>items</code> schema from the merged element type. An empty array yields an empty schema, which matches anything.</li>
      <li>A value that was sometimes null becomes a type array, e.g. <code>"type": ["string", "null"]</code>.</li>
      <li>Mixed non-scalar types become <code>anyOf</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What you have to add by hand</h2>
    <p>
      Add all keywords that encode intent, because no example can show it:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Keyword</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>title</code>, <code>description</code></td><td>Documentation. Editors surface these as hover text and form generators as labels.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>format</code></td><td><code>email</code>, <code>uri</code>, <code>date-time</code>, <code>uuid</code>, <code>ipv4</code>. In 2020-12, format is annotation-only by default. Ajv needs <code>ajv-formats</code> to enforce it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>enum</code> / <code>const</code></td><td>Fixed value sets. The most useful addition, and inference can never make it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>minimum</code>, <code>maximum</code>, <code>minLength</code>, <code>pattern</code></td><td>Range and shape constraints.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>examples</code>, <code>default</code></td><td>Documentation and tooling hints.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>$defs</code> + <code>$ref</code></td><td>Define a repeated shape once, not inline three times.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Refactoring repeated shapes</h2>
    <p>
      The generator puts nested objects inline and does not write <code>$defs</code>, because an inline
      schema is easier to read and to compare with the sample. If the same shape appears in several
      places, move it into <code>$defs</code> once and replace the copies with
      <code>&#123;"$ref": "#/$defs/Address"&#125;</code>. This is a simple edit and makes the schema
      easier to maintain.
    </p>

    <h2>Validating with the result</h2>
    <p>
      This page does not validate a document against a schema. That needs a full validator and belongs
      in your build. The usual next step is Ajv: <code>const validate = ajv.compile(schema)</code>, then
      <code>validate(data)</code> and read <code>validate.errors</code>. In Python, use
      <code>jsonschema.validate(instance, schema)</code>. Both report the failing instance path.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
